Z||000003||theogre||12-28-2000||07:20 PM||||If the TC is failing to lock then the motor will run about 250rpm more than it should at highway speed. Tap the brake and see if the tach jumps up then down when you let off the brake pedal. If it doesn't then the TC is not locking.||||reg||1
Z||000004||RaeQwan||12-29-2000||02:52 AM||||would the TC not locking up cause engine damage?||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-29-2000||09:03 AM||||Probably not engine damage but definatly will bite hard on fuel economy and make the tranny run much hotter than it should. Before you decide how much that matters, read this--&gt; <A HREF="http://www.borg-warner.com/techtips/trans.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.borg-warner.com/techtips/trans.html</A>  <P>It will also increase heat load on the radiator since it also cools the tranny. That could lead to easier overheating if the cooling system isn't 100%.<P>When the TC locks there is almost no heat from the tranny. Go hear--&gt; <A HREF="http://www.motor.com/Motor_Magazine_Back_Issue_Search.ASP" TARGET=_blank>http://www.motor.com/Motor_Magazine_Back_Issue_Search.ASP</A>  select the MAy1999 issues and go to the bottom of the next page that opens. OR If you have acrobat loaded just get this--&gt; <A HREF="http://www.motor.com/MAGAZINE/Pdf/051999_11.pdf" TARGET=_blank>http://www.motor.com/MAGAZINE/Pdf/051999_11.pdf</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||rodmcneill||12-29-2000||09:03 AM||||No, It will not cause engine damage, it will just cause the engine to run at higher rpms. The part that is bad is on-in the transmission.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rodmcneill||12-29-2000||09:16 AM||||Theogre, do you know anyone that has put a temp. sensor and gauge on their auto. Fiero. By the way a great reply, those web sites look very interesting. I will have to check them out tonight when I get home from work.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||12-29-2000||03:57 PM||||Many newer transmissions do have oil temp sensors in them that feed to the ECM. Could it be done to a TH125/440t4/4T60.... I don't see why not. There are several posible ways it could be done. I don't see any reason to do this unless you are racing or towing allot. <P>If you wanted full time readings you'd have to hook up a full gauge similar to the water temp one. <P>But just checking the temp right after a run is easy. Just get a digital pyrometer. It can be a thermocouple type or an IR type. Either is easy to use. stick the thermocouple down the dip stick or just read the pan temp with the IR. You can use either to read engine oil temp as well.<BR>||||reg||1
